Multia Family of MultiClient Desktops

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Low cost Alpha and Windows NT multiclient desktop

o  Embedded software provides powerful PC, UNIX, and alphanumeric
   application interoperability

o  Centralized desktop management removes end user system administration

o  Includes industry standard network protocols

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Digital's Multia family enables users to run PC, UNIX, and alphanumeric
(legacy) applications simultaneously, over industry standard network
protocols. The combination of Alpha technology, Microsoft Windows NT
Workstation V3.5 software, a highly optimized X11R6 X server, and best in
class terminal emulators results in application access with no performance
tradeoffs. The Multia family's embedded configuration management software
enables system administrators to customize and control the Multia user
environment centrally, in both LAN and WAN environments.

The Multia MultiClient Desktop is a network ready device that implements the
application server model of client/server computing. User applications and
data are stored on centrally managed hosts and servers, but are executed
locally during user sessions. UNIX and OpenVMS X applications are displayed
from remote host systems via the X protocol.

Multia Base Software V2.5

Multia Base Software is factory installed on each Multia MultiClient Desktop
configuration. Features include:

o  Windows NT Workstation V3.5 operating system software -- Microsoft's
   Windows NT software is a powerful, multitasking operating system with
   built-in C2 level security features. User applications and files are
   stored permanently on networked servers, rather than local disks. This
   provides a more secure and manageable environment. The optional floppy
   drive configuration is available for exchanging user data with standalone
   PCs for home or portable use. The Windows NT operating system provides
   the Multia system with the following capabilities:

   - The ability to downline load Windows (16-bit) and Windows NT (32-bit)
     applications from networked Windows NT and Novell servers. These
     applications are executed locally.
   - Access to applications and files via LAN Manager, TCP/IP, IPX/SPX,
     NetBEUI, and NetBIOS protocols. DECnet and LAT protocol support will be
     added to Multia Base Software in about six months.
   - Windows NT Workstation operating system is always available in the
     user's Multia system, so only applications and their associated fonts
     and installation files need be downline loaded.

o  Multia Application Manager -- This is the user's primary interface to the
   Multia desktop and is used for accessing local and remote applications.
   Application icons are displayed in a customizable control panel. The list
   of available applications, their location on the network, the transport
   used to access them, and their startup procedures are all predefined by
   the system administrator. End users simply point and click on an icon to
   initiate an application session. The "autostart" option is used to select
   which applications are started automatically when a Multia session is
   begun.

o  Multia Configuration Manager -- The Configuration Manager is a powerful
   tool that enables the system administrator to centrally customize and
   manage the Multia end user environment. For example, system
   administrators can create workgroups of Multia users who have common
   application requirements and skill sets. Workgroup customizations are
   defined by the system administrator and include:

   - Which applications are available and where they are located on the
     network.
   - Which hardware options are enabled or disabled (e.g., the floppy drive
     settings for keyboard and desktop).
   - Network settings such as IP addresses, client services for NetWare, and
     network drivers.

   The system administrator also can unlock any Multia feature that enables
   the end user to define their own customizations. Both workgroup and
   individual customization data can reside on any networked machine
   accessible from a Multia system, including another Multia desktop. The
   configuration data is stored in a format that can be edited by the user
   and is easy to back up and restore. System administrators can use their
   own Multia systems to store department configuration data and then back
   up to a networked server.

   The Configuration Manager supports both LAN and WAN environments and
   provides password protection. The STATUS feature enables a system
   administrator to query any Multia system in the environment in order to
   obtain information on network counters, which applications are being
   accessed, and system box configuration data.

o  Local Terminal Emulators -- The local terminal emulator, included in the
   Multia Base Software, provides full VT340 graphics, VT420 text, and
   Tektronics 4010/4014 terminal emulation.

o  Reference Desk -- The Reference Desk provides users with online
   information about Multia systems, including:
   - Graphical user tutorial for getting started.
   - Problem solving.
   - Context sensitive help.
   - System administrator's information and online help.
   - Information on software and hardware options, including graphical
     installation instructions.

o  32-bit Mosaic software provides a mechanism to access the Internet.

o  Local language support -- Software supports user-selectable setup in
   English, French, Spanish, and German.

Multia System Administrator's Kit

The Multia System Administrator's Kit provides additional tools for the
system administrator. One kit per installation is required. This kit
includes:

o  CD-ROM media.
o  Multia Software for System Administrator's V2.5. This software includes:
   - Multia Base Software V2.5.
   - Host kits. The software can be installed on any machine running the
     following operating systems, for the purposes of storage or backup: DEC
     OSF/1 and ULTRIX systems, IBM AIX, HP-UZ, SCO UNIX, Solaris, and
     Windows NT servers.
   - Multia required fonts.
   - Configuration templates for creating workgroups.
   - Online systems administrator's tutorial and hard copy System
     Administrator's Guide.

Multia X Server Option V3.0

The X Server option is a local application that provides interoperability
with UNIX and OpenVMS X applications. This X Server has been highly
optimized for the Multia system's graphics processor. The resulting
performance is equivalent to best in class X terminals -- far exceeding
standard PC X Server software performance. The Multia X Server is fully
integrated into the Configuration Manager so that all of its features can be
remotely customized and managed for workgroups and individual Multia users.
Features include:

o  Optimized X11.R6 X server.
o  Local OSF Motif Window Manager.
o  X Image Extension.
o  NFS client software.
o  Enhanced TCP/IP protocol adding support for dynamic routing.
o  Font support for X Font Server, SNF, BDF and PCF font formats.
o  Font failover.
o  Cut and paste between X Windows and Microsoft Windows.
o  XDM host chooser.
o  Backing store and save under.
o  Shape extension.

With the Multia X Server option, users can run UNIX and OpenVMS applications
alongside Windows, Windows NT, and legacy applications. All these
applications can be accessed simultaneously, using different network
protocols.

Multia Serial X Protocol Option V3.0

Used in conjunction with the Multia X Server option, this software provides
X session connectivity over a serial line.

Multia System Box

o  166 MHz Alpha processor with high performance PCI based graphics
   accelerator.
o  PCI internal bus.
o  2 PCMCIA slots support type 1 or 2 cards.
o  PCI option slot.
o  CD quality audio input and output, plus built-in microphone and speaker.
o  Kensington lock ready.
o  SCSI-2 connector option.
o  Floppy drive option.
o  340 Mbyte SCSI hard disk -- The internal disk is used for Multia embedded
   software, configuration data, fonts, and required application
   installation files.
o  Graphical hardware installation instructions.

Communications

o  The Multia system includes two RS232 serial lines, one with full modem
   support and one bidirectional parallel port.
o  Autosensing PCI based ethernet connections for Thinwire, Thickwire, or
   Twisted Pair (10BaseT).

Memory

o  24 Mbytes of 72 pin industry standard SIMM memory, expandable to 256
   Mbytes.
o  ECC memory supports error detection and correction.

Physical Characteristics

o  Height: 7.1 centimeters (2.8 inches).
o  Width: 31.7 centimeters (12.5 inches).
o  Depth: 31.7 centimeters (12.5 inches).
o  Weight: 13.5 pounds (6.1 kg) maximum.

Power Support: Electrical Power Cord

o  100-120V or auto range.
o  100-240V with detachable power cord.
o  88-264V single phase, three-wire design.
o  47-63 Hz frequency.

Regulatory Compliance

o  CISPR Class B compliance.
o  FCC Class B.

Keyboards

o  The system supports PCXAL (101/102 keys, PC layout) and LK411 (108 keys,
   Digital layout) keyboards. Additional keyboards planned include UNIX,
   Sun, and HP style.

Monitors

o  Multisync color and greyscale monitors from 640 x 480 to 1280 x 1024
   resolutions, at 60 to 75 hz refresh rate for CRT. Automatic screen saver
   mode for energy conservation.

BENEFITS

o  The ability to access multiple application types simultaneously improves
   user productivity through multitasking.

o  Central desktop management simplifies the user environment and results in
   a lower cost of operation compared to PCs or workstations.

o  Interoperability features such as embedded network protocols and UNIX,
   PC, and legacy application access provide seamless access to multiple
   networks and applications.

o  Superior end user ease of use features are provided via the Application
   Manager.

o  The system is optimized for workgroup and client/server computing.

o  Exceptional performance is provided via Alpha technology.

o  It provides a more secure environment than networked PCs.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The Multia MultiClient Desktop, Base Software, and System Administrator's
Kit are available to order now. Fulfillments will begin December 6, 1994.
The Multia X Server and Serial X protocol options will be available in March
1995.

The Multia system box can be expanded to support up to 256 Mbyte memory.
Memory is 72 pin industry standard SIMMs. SIMMs must be added in pairs.

