AdvantageCluster Compute Server Model 5000 for Technical Computing

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Latest member of the AdvantageCluster Compute Server family, the Model
   5000 features a rackmounted parallel cluster equipped with four
   AlphaServer 2100 4/200 (or 4/275) RM symmetric multi-processing (SMP)
   compute nodes

o  The AlphaServer 2100 nodes in the Model 5000 ship in single and quad
   processor configurations, offering an industry-leading range of price and
   performance points, and delivering unique scalability from a single
   system

o  The AlphaServer 2100 compute nodes in the Model 5000 are interconnected
   with GIGAswitch, Digital's award-winning, low-latency, non-blocking,
   optical crossbar switch

o  The Model 5000 includes the TechAdvantage suite of parallel development
   and optimization tools, Parallel Virtual Machine (PVM) software, and
   POLYCENTER system management software -- all factory-installed and
   configured

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

With the AdvantageCluster Compute Server (ACCS) series of UNIX clusters,
Digital provides a range of technical computing solutions to address the
most difficult of compute-intensive applications. The Model 5000 offers
flexible and scalable supercomputing at affordable prices.

The Model 5000 consists of four AlphaServer 2100 RM systems connected with
the GIGAswitch product, the industry's only FDDI crossbar switch offering an
unprecedented 3.6 Gbits per second bandwidth. Each AlphaServer 2100 arrives
in either a single or quad processor configuration, with either 128 or 512
Mbytes of memory. A standard Model 5000 delivers the power of up to 16 Alpha
processors from two 19-inch cabinet bays. The system can be further expanded
with the addition of any number of AlphaServer 2100 nodes for unmatched
scalability and flexibility.

Supercomputer users who are downsizing must decide whether a UNIX cluster
("farm"), massively parallel processor (MPP), or symmetric multiprocessor
(SMP) best meets their needs. Each approach defines its own style of
computing. The AdvantageCluster Compute Server Model 5000 offers the
benefits of UNIX clustering and SMP compute styles today in a single system.

Family Positioning

The AdvantageCluster Compute Server family now has five members. The Models
1000, 2000, 3000, and 4000 use increasing numbers of clustered DEC 3000
workstations to deliver the desired compute horse-power. All models run
applications written for DEC OSF/1.

Parallel applications can be implemented using Fortran 77 and Parallel
Virtual Machines (PVM) software, or using the the DEC High Performance
Fortran 90 compiler. This new compiler automates inter-processor task
distribution, insulating the programmer from the parallel nature of the
server.

The different AdvantageCluster Compute Server models are positioned as
follows:

Model 1000	    Entry-level parallel compute server, low price point,
                    4-node cluster using Ethernet interconnect, parallel
                    runtime environment.

Model 2000	    Best price/performance parallel compute server, dense
                    packaging, 4-node cluster, GIGAswitch interconnect,
                    parallel runtime environment.

Model 3000	    Best performance in the industry from a four-node
                    parallel cluster, GIGAswitch interconnect, parallel
                    runtime environment.

Model 4000	    Highest performance of any size cluster in the industry,
                    32 compute nodes, GIGAswitch interconnect, parallel
                    runtime environment.

Model 5000	    Nearly best price/performance at 4x1 (four nodes x one
                    uni-processor AlphaServer 2100 per node), best
                    expandability at 4 CPUs to 16 CPUs in the same cabinet,
                    best price/performance at 4x4 (four nodes x one four-CPU
                    AlphaServer 2100 per node), uses latest technology, is
                    the only integrated parallel software development and
                    runtime environment, SMP and parallel clustering compute
                    styles in a single system, low-risk entry into
                    supercomputing.

BENEFITS

Parallel Cluster Systems

To remain competitive, industries are looking to computer modeling and
simulation techniques to shorten design and test cycles, raise product
quality, and lower product development costs.

Clusters, or "farms" of standard compute engines, working in parallel, are a
perfect solution for the world of computer modeling and simulation. In fact,
there is now a trend among supercomputer owners to downsize their vector
supercomputing environments to parallel cluster systems to take advantage of
the:

o  Much lower price and cost of ownership.
o  Conformance to the client/server compute model.
o  Greatly reduced down-time because of the redundancy and reliability
   inherent in parallel systems.
o  Hot-swap maintenance capability in the case of node failures.
o  Automatic load-balancing of multiple jobs across available nodes in the
   cluster.

Examples of technical computing problems being solved more affordably, yet
as quickly, on clusters include:

o  Finite element analysis
o  Computational fluid dynamics
o  Genetic engineering
o  Computational chemistry
o  Molecular modeling
o  Electronic circuit simulation
o  Stock portfolio analysis

These problems often involve extremely large data sets and may take hours,
days, or even weeks of computing.

The parallel server configuration allows previously irreconcilable roles to
be played by a single machine:

o  The execution time of individual technical computing jobs is accelerated
   to achieve supercomputer throughput, by running the job in parallel
   across several server nodes.

o  The server will service large numbers of requests from applications
   running on client workstations, while simultaneously running large
   parallel jobs.

o  Client workstations (from various vendors) can work interactively with
   jobs running on a single cluster node, or jobs running in parallel across
   all cluster nodes.

Greater job throughput, more flexibility, and better compute resource
utilization are benefits available to users with clustered configurations.

AdvantageCluster Compute Server Model 5000

o  Supercomputer downsizing can be achieved at an attractive price.

o  With the AlphaServer 2100 leading the industry in price-performance, the
   Model 5000 is highly competitive compared with other multi-processor
   implementations.

o  More flexible client-server job handling is possible compared to
   traditional batch-driven supercomputers. Numerically intensive compute
   jobs can run in parallel across all compute nodes, while the same nodes
   share less demanding job requests from the network. Thanks to X Windows,
   users can interact with numerically-intensive jobs running in parallel on
   the Model 5000.

o  With High-Performance Fortran 90 included with the Model 5000, code can
   be developed, compiled, and debugged on a single Model 5000 node, then
   run fast, in parallel, over the whole cluster, without requiring a change
   in source code. Software developers can therefore move easily between
   software development and runtime tasks. No other parallel cluster system
   offers shared development and runtime environments. In addition, software
   developed for the AdvantageCluster Models 1000, 2000, 3000, and 4000 will
   run without modification on the Model 5000 SMP machines.

   The AdvantageCluster Compute Server Model 5000/200 scales from an
   entry-level four-node system of uni-processors delivering 760 Mflops
   (expandable to a four-node system of quad processors delivering 3,040
   Mflops). This is easily upgradable to a 34-node (maximum) Model 5000/275
   system with quad Alpha 21064A processors delivering 37.4 Gflops of
   performance. Custom systems can be configured to satisfy the most
   power-hungry applications.

o  The Model 5000 has a small footprint, housing unmatched power and storage
   capacity in two standard cabinets (70 inches high, 19 inches wide).

o  With GIGAswitch interconnect, the AdvantageCluster Compute Server Model
   5000 offers the fastest FDDI inter-processor communication rates. At 100
   Mbits per second per compute node (half-duplex), the GIGAswitch product
   can switch an aggregate of 3.6 Gbits per second. Switching connection
   setup (latency) takes about 17 microseconds.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The AdvantageCluster Compute Server Model 5000 is available now.

Digital offers a service to quote, build, and integrate AdvantageCluster
Systems as large as required. The advantage of parallel cluster computing is
that processors can be added while maintaining linear performance gains.

The Model 5000 ships with the TechAdvantage suite of parallel software
development and optimization tools (including PVM) to quickly make
supercomputing accessible to (upsizing) users and to simplify the transition
from vector supercomputers to parallel cluster systems (see April 12, 1994
issue of Customer Update). The Model 5000 also ships with Digital's
