MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 V1.5 with New Prices and License Options

HIGHLIGHTS

o  New concurrent user license options with attractively priced 25, 50, and
   100 user increments

o  Improved user management

o  New MailWorks Client for SunOS

o  New cc:Mail Connection for cc:Mail V2.0 Client

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 (previously called DEC MAILworks for DEC OSF/1) is a
mail system, for both large and small workgroups, that ties easily to the
enterprise messaging system. MailWorks utilizes a flexible and scalable
client/server design that lets users start small and easily grow to meet
current and future requirements. The MailWorks, DEC OSF/1, and Alpha
combination delivers a high performance server environment that supports a
large number of users with heterogeneous desktops.

MailWorks supports a variety of mail clients including TeamLinks for Windows
and Macintosh; MicroSoft Mail for Windows; Lotus cc:Mail for Windows; and
MailWorks Clients for MS-DOS, Motif, DEC OSF/1, and SunOS.

The MailWorks Server offers a choice of messaging transports including
built-in connectivity to UNIX SendMail, which provides a seamless connection
to the Internet and other SMTP environments. MailWorks connects directly to
Digital's leadership X.400 Message Transport Agent, MAILbus 400; and to
Digital's X.500 Directory Services. In addition, MailWorks can exchange
messages with Digital's VMSmail. MAILbus 400 and DEC X.500 Directory
Services (SPD 40.77.xx) are optional products that are purchased and
installed separately from MailWorks.

New Features In MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 V1.5

MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 V1.5 includes new features that allow the system
administrator to easily manage MailWorks user accounts. A simple script
prompts the administrator for new user account information. The script also
allows modification and deletion of user accounts.

MailWorks Delivery/Non-Delivery Notices and Read Reciepts now include the
original message subject line to facilitate correlation to the original
message.

In addition, an integrated directory services lookup feature has been added
to the MailWorks Client for DEC OSF/1 similar to the directory lookup
capability already existing in the TeamLinks, Microsoft Mail, and Lotus
cc:Mail clients.

Users can now employ the following TeamLinks features with the MailWorks for
DEC OSF/1 Server, gaining greater compatability with the other TeamLinks
file servers:

o  Set the server account password from TeamLinks.
o  Include blind CC (carbon copies) addresses in the message header.
o  Take advantage of Read/Send message handling preferences.

Also, extensive improvements have been made to MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 to
increase reliability and offer high performance.

A new MailWorks Motif Client for SunOS is available with V1.5. This client
is consistent with the existing DEC OSF/1 Motif client, including the new
integrated directory lookup feature. The SunOS client requires SunOS 4.1.3
with Patch Level Open Windows V3.0 100444-58 and a suitable Sun workstation
with at least 32 Mbytes of memory. The Sun workstation must be connected to
the MailWorks server node via TCP/IP. The MailWorks SunOS client is packaged
with the MailWorks CD-ROM and can be installed via the network. The
MailWorks SunOS Client has its own license and one is required for each
installation of the client.

New License Options and Prices

New license options for concurrent users have been added with increments of
1 user, 25 users, 50 users, and 100 users. The increments are individually
priced to make these options attractive for large and small configurations.
As a result, the capacity license price has been increased to correctly
position it for installations with more than 100 users.

The new prices provide competitive configurations for small workgroups and
workgroups of hundreds of users, allowing users to grow the size of their
workgroups as their needs increase. The new license options for the
MailWorks server, when combined with TeamLinks mail clients, deliver
competitive prices. For users who already have Lotus cc:Mail and Microsoft
Mail, MailWorks is a very attractive option for taking advantage of the
benefits of client/server technology, including robust mail features,
scalability, simplified system management, and increased performance.

Directory Services for MailWorks

Directory Services for MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 is provided through the
InfoBroker Server, which is included in the license for MailWorks. The
InfoBroker Server (SPD 53.32.xx) provides a local directory with up to 5000
entries. It also provides the functions of an X.500 Directory User Agent for
accessing Digital's DEC X.500 Directory Server. The DEC X.500 Directory
Server should be used whenever an organization wishes to implement a higher
capacity, high-performance network directory service. TeamLinks, the
MailWorks clients, and the InfoBroker Client for Microsoft Windows all have
access to the directory service provided through the InfoBroker Server.

Lotus cc:Mail Connection for MailWorks

The cc:Mail Connection for MailWorks provides a significant improvement over
cc:Mail LAN-based file sharing technology, without requiring a change to the
user interface. Lotus cc:Mail product users simply need to install the
cc:Mail Connection for MailWorks on their PCs and have an account on a
MailWorks server within their LAN to be able to use their existing cc:Mail
client with MailWorks. The cc:Mail Connection product includes the necessary
software and a Software Replication License that allows you to install the
cc:Mail Connection on as many PCs as required. However, the MailWorks Server
must have the appropriate licenses for the required number of concurrent
users.

Basic cc:Mail product operations include:

o  Create and delete folders
o  Send, read, create, delete and copy messages
o  Attach text and binary files
o  Reply to and forward messages
o  Search, specify filtering rules

In addition, the MailWorks Server provides X.500 Directory Services and
personal address book directory service.

The benefits of using the Digital MailWorks/MAILbus messaging backbone in
place of LAN mail file-sharing technology include:

o  Improved dependability of basic mail services, such as message delivery
   and receipt.

o  Enhanced security and reduced potential for data corruption of messages.

o  Expansive directory services to ease communications across the
   heterogeneous mail enterprise.

o  Ease of wide-area connectivity and gateway service access (EDI, X.400,
   Internet, etc.).

o  Lower cost of LAN mail connectivity and enterprise integration.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

MailWorks for DEC OSF/1 V1.5 will be available on December 30, 1994. The
cc:Mail Connection V1.0 will be available in March 1995. New license options
and prices are effective now.

