Composer by IEF

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Provides the ability to build client/server applications for a variety of
   platforms, operating systems, databases, transaction processing monitors,
   and languages

o  Delivers 100 percent generation of data, complex logic, and presentation
   application components from high-level graphical specifications, across
   both servers and clients

o  Supports generation for ACMS, DECForms, Rdb, and ORACLE today

o  Forms the infrastructure for future object technology applications

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

Texas Instruments' Composer by IEF (Information Engineering Facility)
software is a leader in building scalable and flexible applications for
businesses of all sizes. The software is fully compliant with its
predecessor, the IEF program, which is installed in 50 percent of the
Fortune 1000 companies.

The Composer enables business users and software developers not only to
build, but also re-use client and/or server application components in
development, production, and decision-support environments.

Decision-support systems are built easily due to the Composer's support of
Advanced GUI++. Users can simply "drag" images to change the live data.

The Composer features multi-language support to form a base for product
nationalization or localization, including development, application design,
and generation in local languages. This architecture offers users in Europe,
Africa, the Pacific Rim, South America, and Canada easy regeneration of
applications in the language appropriate for foreign branch locations.

The Composer software supports full distribution of applications across
multiple server platforms, including Digital's OpenVMS and Rdb, Oracle V7,
HP-UX, OS/2, Sun Solaris, RS/6000 AIX, MVS, Sequent, Siemens Nixdorf's RM,
and TANDEM software. It is the first integrated development tool to offer
transaction processing, based on the distribution of client/server
applications, from a diagram of specifications for design and maintenance.

Applications for Digital systems may be generated from Composer running on
workstations from other vendors such as Sun, IBM, HP, and PCs running
Windows 3.1.

Composer is a suite of three toolsets:

o  Developers' Workstation Toolset -- Software developers use this software
   to both gather and input requirements for a given part of a business
   application, including GUI applications. Graphical diagrams are used to
   create a model of the business specifications, which then turns into
   system applications that automate processes.  Since the Composer
   generates 100 percent of the application code for the targeted platform
   (client/server or traditional architectures) from these diagrams, the
   developer builds applications independent of the target implementation.

o  Client/Server or Host-based Encyclopedia Toolset -- Developers who employ
   the Developers' Workstation Toolset use this software to manage and
   re-use application components and models. The software's encyclopedia-
   type architecture provides a scalable environment where client or server
   components can be added or used as needed by the workstation developer.
   Functionally, the Encyclopedia Toolset manages and administers
   information stored in a relational database.

o  Implementation Toolset -- The Implementation Toolset software runs on a
   computer configured with the final target platform (OpenVMS, OS/2,
   Windows, MVS, HP-UX, etc.). The product ensures the automation of the
   compilation, linking, and installation of the Composer-developed
   application. By providing a mechanism for defining all the variable
   elements (DBMS, operating system, etc.) of the target system
   environments, the Implementation Toolset creates the framework for
   repeatable installation processes. The software also ensures that future
   modifications of the application match the specification and are
   installed correctly and consistently.

BENEFITS

o  Provides rapid client/server application development.

o  Forms the infrastructure for taking advantage of object technology in the
   future.

o  Protects development investment; users are not locked into specific
   client/server models since the Composer builds applications for a
   multitude of languages, environments, and platforms.

o  Allows the addition of clients and increasing transaction volumes in a
   remote data client/server environment, without degrading system response
   time.

o  Insulates developers from network protocols and other technology.

o  Eliminates duplication of work.

o  Is hardware independent.

o  Supports multi-user and multi-team development environments.

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

Composer by IEF software is available now through Texas Instruments' sales
offices.

The earlier IEF release of Implementation Toolset and Client/Server
Encyclopedia software is available now for Digital's VAX servers running
OpenVMS and Oracle, Rdb, ACMS, or DECForms software. The new, more robust
Composer release is currently being tested for OpenVMS VAX and AXP platforms
and is slated for availability in March l995.

